  <h1>India’s Digital Public Infrastructure (DPI) — A Global Model</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>India’s Digital Public Infrastructure (DPI) — built around Aadhaar, UPI, and DigiLocker — has become a global benchmark for inclusive digital governance. In 2024, the Indian Express highlighted how DPI has transformed service delivery, financial inclusion, and citizen–state interaction. India’s presidency of the G20 (2023) amplified this achievement, as many developing nations expressed interest in adopting the Indian model.
________________________________________
What is DPI?
•	Definition: Open, interoperable digital platforms that serve as public goods.
•	Core Elements in India:
o	Aadhaar: Unique ID for authentication.
o	UPI: Real-time payments system.
o	DigiLocker: Digital storage for certificates.
o	Co-Win, FASTag, ONDC: Sectoral applications built on DPI backbone.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Achievements<br/>1.	Financial Inclusion<br/>o	Jan Dhan–Aadhaar–Mobile (JAM) Trinity connected millions to banking.<br/>o	UPI crossed 10 billion monthly transactions in 2023, making it world’s largest payment platform.<br/>2.	Welfare Delivery<br/>o	Direct Benefit Transfers (DBT) reduced leakages; >₹25 lakh crore transferred since 2014.<br/>o	Subsidies for LPG, pensions, and scholarships streamlined.<br/>3.	Global Recognition<br/>o	World Bank praised DPI as “transformative digital infrastructure.”<br/>o	India assisting countries like Philippines, Mauritius, and Morocco in replicating models.<br/>4.	Economic Growth<br/>o	Enabled startups, fintech ecosystem, and e-commerce growth.<br/>o	ONDC promises to democratize digital marketplaces.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Challenges<br/>•	Digital Divide: 40% of rural households lack internet access.<br/>•	Privacy Concerns: Data protection remains weak despite Digital Personal Data Protection Act (2023).<br/>•	Cybersecurity Risks: Increased threat of fraud, phishing, and identity theft.<br/>•	Exclusion Errors: Aadhaar-based authentication failures still reported in welfare schemes.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Global Context<br/>•	EU: Digital identity projects but fragmented across states.<br/>•	US: No national-level ID system.<br/>•	China: Advanced but highly centralized and surveillance-heavy.<br/>India’s DPI offers a middle path: scalable yet democratic, balancing access with safeguards (though gaps remain).<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): E-governance, DBT, citizen-centric services.<br/>•	GS-III (Economy & Tech): Fintech revolution, digital economy.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Digital public goods as tools of inclusive development.”<br/>o	“Balancing efficiency with privacy in governance.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Soft power through technology diplomacy.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Bridge the Digital Divide: Expand BharatNet and affordable smartphones.<br/>2.	Strengthen Data Governance: Independent Data Protection Board for oversight.<br/>3.	Cybersecurity Measures: National CERT capacity and user education.<br/>4.	Global DPI Diplomacy: Position India as leader in digital cooperation forums.<br/>5.	Inclusive Design: Multilingual, disability-friendly digital platforms.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s DPI is a revolution in governance and service delivery, empowering millions and inspiring global adoption. The challenge ahead is to deepen inclusivity, strengthen safeguards, and use this model as a tool of global influence. For UPSC aspirants, DPI represents the fusion of governance, economy, and international cooperation.</p>
